##
    Changing a sequence while walking it.

    This program does not compile. A 'loop each' reads its sequence's length once, when the loop
    begins, so inserting into it does not lengthen the walk and removing from it leaves the
    walk reaching past the end. Neither is what the line looks like it does.

    A range loop is the opposite and says so: its bound is read on every turn, so a bound that
    moves is a bound that moves. That is why only 'loop each' is refused here.
##

# Nothing here is run, so nothing reads what it declares and nothing keeps what it
# works out. That is the shape of every negative, and not the lesson of this one.
# ignore PC0409 in file
# ignore PC0412 in file

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Growing();
        Program.Shrinking();
        Program.EmptyingIt();
        Program.WalkingWhatIsNotASequence();
        Program.WalkingOneThatMayNotBeThere();
        Program.AStepThatStaysPut();
        Program.AStepPointingAway();
        Program.ABoundAlreadyBehind();
        Program.AnExclusiveBoundAlreadyReached();
        Console.WriteLine(Program.NothingEndsIt());
    end function

    ##
        A loop with no condition and no way out. Two things are said about it, and they are
        different things.

        PC0406 is an opinion: nothing in the body breaks, yields, or throws, so nothing written
        here will ever stop it. That alone is not an error — a program may mean to run until it
        is stopped from outside.

        PC0404 is the error, and it is about the promise rather than the loop: this function
        says it yields an integer and has no path that does.
    ##
    integer function NothingEndsIt()
        loop
            Console.WriteLine("and on, and on");
        end loop
    end function

    # Inserting. The walk was already measured, so the new element is never reached.
    function Growing()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        loop each amount in amounts
            amounts.Insert(amount * 10);
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count);
    end function

    # Removing. The walk still runs to the length the set had, which is now past its end.
    function Shrinking()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        loop each amount in amounts
            amounts.Remove(amount);
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count);
    end function

    ##
        The same rule reaches a field, and reaches whichever member changes the set — Insert,
        InsertAt, Remove, RemoveAt, and Clear. A member that yields a new set instead leaves
        the original alone and is not in question.
    ##
    function EmptyingIt()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        loop each amount in amounts
            amounts.Clear();
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count);
    end function

    ##
        A 'loop each' walks a set or a string, and nothing else. A number is the one people
        reach for, meaning "this many times" — which is what the range loop below the refusal
        is for, and what the message names.
    ##
    function WalkingWhatIsNotASequence()
        loop each turn in 3
            Console.WriteLine(turn);
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        A step of zero never moves the counter, so the loop runs forever. PC0413 is an opinion
        for the same reason PC0406 above it is: running forever is a shape a program may mean,
        and this one is exact about what it does rather than unpredictable. What it is not is
        anything somebody wrote on purpose with a bound in front of it.
    ##
    function AStepThatStaysPut()
        loop for at = 1 to 10 stepby 0
            Console.WriteLine(at);
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        A step pointing away from the bound, so the body never runs at all. That is a warning
        rather than an opinion: the lines inside were written to be run, and nothing about the
        loop's shape is a matter of taste. An editor dims it, which is what it does with
        everything nothing reaches.

        Counting down is not in question — 'loop for at = 10 to 1 stepby -1' is the ordinary
        way to write it, and it is the step and the bound disagreeing that is reported.
    ##
    function AStepPointingAway()
        loop for at = 1 to 10 stepby -1
            Console.WriteLine(at);
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        The same thing with no step written, which is a step of one. This is the plainest form
        of it and the likeliest to be typed: a bound behind where the counting starts.
    ##
    function ABoundAlreadyBehind()
        loop for at = 5 to 3
            Console.WriteLine(at);
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        And with 'until', where the bound is one to stop before rather than one to reach.
        Starting on it is already past it, so the body never runs — where the same two numbers
        written with 'to' would run once.
    ##
    function AnExclusiveBoundAlreadyReached()
        loop for at = 5 until 5
            Console.WriteLine(at);
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        An optional holding a set is not a set, for the same reason it is not anything it
        holds. The walk would have to decide what to do about the absence, and the two answers
        — walk nothing, or stop — are different enough that the program has to say which.
    ##
    function WalkingOneThatMayNotBeThere()
        integer[]? amounts;

        loop each amount in amounts
            Console.WriteLine(amount);
        end loop
    end function

end model
